{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"30c814d0766f2ca2","type":"text","text":"“We migrate incrementally using the Strangler Fig pattern. \nWe identify domain boundaries using DDD, extract low-risk services first, route traffic via an API gateway, and ensure each service owns its data. For cross-service transactions we use Saga and event-driven communication. Observability and CI/CD are mandatory before scaling microservices\"\n","x":-20,"y":-400,"width":605,"height":160},
		{"id":"c5b036b9baf18e3c","type":"text","text":"## Barclays:\n#### [[Strangler Fig Pattern]]:\nDivided the Apply journey into using Domain Driven Design :\n- Pre Decision services: Consumer Service, Offers Service, Orchestration Service\n- Decision: Core Apply service\n- Post Decision: Notification Service, Compliance Service, Loyalty Service, external service connecting to Rewards API\nSeparate Database for their sync operations for different domains.\n\n### [[Side car pattern]]\nDivided the monolithic into different services by identifying different channels of Applying for CC.\nOther services/Wrapper Services like Authentication Service, Validation Service, Notification service.\n","x":-20,"y":-160,"width":480,"height":320,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"20a49bebf70eb61b","type":"text","text":"![[Monolithic To Microservices]]","x":-40,"y":-960,"width":560,"height":480},
		{"id":"537e1a73dd208f43","type":"text","text":"#### How do you handle failures?\n\n“When migrating from a monolith to microservices, we assume failures will happen and design for them. We roll out incrementally using the Strangler Fig pattern so we can quickly route traffic back to the monolith. Services use timeouts, retries with backoff, and circuit breakers to avoid cascading failures. All operations are idempotent to handle retries safely. For multi-service workflows, we use Saga patterns with compensating transactions instead of distributed transactions. Finally, we rely heavily on observability—metrics, tracing, and logs—to detect failures early and recover quickly”","x":780,"y":-580,"width":600,"height":260,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"e363f61ba3c61f5c","type":"text","text":"### Mastercard\n\n#### [[SAGA pattern]]\n\nA big monolithic application is divided into modular monolithic and then to microservices.\n- UI-> JSP, [[React]] & Angular [[Micro frontend]] divided using Feature driven design.\n- EOP-portal: a wrapper backend application that acts as ETL service (Extract Transform Load). This does not have access to DB, does not have access to [[Kafka]]. Only Extract data from UI, process/transforms them and post(load) the data to core service for main processing. This has basic validations and fast fail circuit breakers.\n- EOP Application: Core service where heavy processing is done. It has a lot of Transactional processing. It is first point of [[SAGA pattern]] for Transactional processing.\n- Offers Management service: This only has async kafka event listeners that processes the data and sends the data back to listeners on other services.\n- Phoenix: This has other core processing being handled. This is another big chunk after EOP application from big Monolithic application. It has REST calls for UI and Kafka Event Listeners. It has separate DB\n- Phoenix UI: Interacts with phoenix backend. Update the values after Async Listener calls and DB fetches.","x":-20,"y":190,"width":480,"height":320,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"fd8a3e73d4c153be","type":"text","text":"### Mastercard\nSDP application\n\nModular Monolithic\nIt has internal processing: tokenization/detokenization and Key Rotation and Last DeToken Update. Everything is inside a single application but code is divided using modular monolithic. Multithreading is used so as to sepearte out operations within the services.","x":-20,"y":540,"width":440,"height":320,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"3d0b23207f4513c5","type":"text","text":"#### How do you handle DB consistency?\n\nWith PostgreSQL, we rely on ACID transactions and strong consistency within a service boundary. Each microservice owns its database schema, and we use local transactions for atomicity. For cross-service consistency, we avoid distributed transactions and use Saga patterns with either orchestration or choreography, combined with idempotent APIs[^1] and [[transactional outbox patterns]]\n\nIn Cassandra, we handle DB consistency using tunable consistency levels. Each service owns its tables and keyspace, and we use QUORUM or LOCAL_QUORUM for strong read-write consistency. For correctness-critical operations, we use Lightweight Transactions (LWT) with conditional updates. Cross-service consistency is handled using Saga patterns and idempotent writes rather than distributed transactions.\n\n[^1]: An **Idempotent API** ensures that performing the same operation multiple times yields the same result as performing it once. It prevents duplicate charges, double-shipped orders, or corrupted data.\n","x":790,"y":-280,"width":590,"height":300,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"60eb946faa02374a","type":"text","text":"#### How do you avoid downtime?\n\nWe avoid downtime by migrating incrementally using the Strangler Fig pattern rather than a big-bang rewrite. All traffic goes through an API Gateway, which lets us gradually route requests to new microservices while keeping the monolith live. We use backward-compatible APIs, feature flags, and canary deployments so changes can be rolled out and rolled back instantly. During the transition, we use dual writes and shadow reads to validate correctness before fully switching traffic. Combined with strong monitoring, this allows us to migrate without service interruption.","x":790,"y":60,"width":590,"height":260,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"29068eead3d544c6","type":"text","text":"### When would you stop migration?\nWe stop migration when the primary goals—independent scaling, faster deployments, and clear ownership—are achieved, and further splitting no longer provides measurable value. In practice, that means leaving stable, low-change modules in the monolith and avoiding over-fragmentation when operational complexity, latency, and cost begin to outweigh the benefits. Migration should always be driven by business and operational ROI, not by the idea that everything must become a microservice.","x":790,"y":360,"width":590,"height":240,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"67fff588c3a530f3","type":"text","text":"![[Monolithic To Microservices#Step-by-step How zero downtime is actually achieved]]","x":1460,"y":44,"width":540,"height":556},
